What ZangbandTK has done to these tilesets
==========================================

All four sets came from Angband and none of them was drawn for this game, so
none of them had art for anything ZangbandTK adds.  Rather than leave the
imported bestiary and the whole wilderness rendering as ASCII letters among the
pictures, we have changed them.  This file says exactly how, per tileset, so
that anybody -- a packager, a tileset author, somebody diffing against upstream
Angband -- can see what is ours without reading the pixels.

Two kinds of change, and the difference matters
-----------------------------------------------

**A mapping** adds a `.prf` file saying "this thing looks like that cell".  It
adds no art and alters no image: the picture was always in the sheet, and
nothing but a lookup table has changed.

**A modification** writes pixels into the sheet.

Both are recorded below.  The distinction is not pedantry -- it is the whole of
whether a licence that forbids modification has been respected.


Per tileset
-----------

**gervais (32x32) -- mapped, and one row added.**

  Angband maps 1151 of this sheet's 3840 cells.  Another 1239 unmapped cells
  hold art, and a contiguous block of it -- sheet rows 23 to 25 -- is a
  wilderness terrain set that Angband never had a use for.  `feat-ztk.prf`
  points thirteen ZangbandTK features at cells that were already there.

  This used to say the image was byte-identical to Angband's, and it stopped
  being true on 10 September 2026, when `scripts/tiles/make-services.py` drew
  the service buildings Gervais has no art for into **one new row** at the
  bottom of `32x32.png` (`serv-ztk.prf`).  The Statue and Figurine Stores'
  doors joined them in 3.130.0 and 3.131.0.  Every cell above that row is still Angband's, byte for byte.

**adam-bolt (16x16), nomad (16x16), old (8x8) -- modified.**

  These had no wilderness art to map, so it was generated:
  `scripts/tiles/make-terrain.py` appends **one new row of thirty tiles** to the
  bottom of each sheet -- ten terrain features in three lighting variants -- and
  writes the matching `feat-ztk.prf`.

  Nothing already in those sheets is touched.  The row is added below the
  existing art, so every original cell keeps its coordinates and every original
  pixel its value.  The colours are sampled from the sheet being extended, so
  the new tiles are in that tileset's own palette rather than in one we chose.

  The script is deterministic and idempotent: re-running it reproduces the same
  bytes, and reclaims the row it wrote last time rather than adding another.

**All four -- mapped, for the monsters.**

  `graf-ztk.prf` in each directory gives the 234 imported monsters that resemble
  an Angband monster the picture of that monster, so they are not letters in a
  tiled game.  Generated by `scripts/tiles/borrow-tiles.py`.  Uniques are
  deliberately left out: a borrowed portrait on a named character asserts an
  identity it does not have, which is the rule in DEC-18.

  That rule used to be written down here as "Oberon of Amber came out wearing
  the Mouth of Sauron's face".  True of automatic borrowing, but a poor account
  of the policy, because the Neon set already ships Oberon, Dworkin and Mandor
  sharing a tile with the Mouth of Sauron -- all four are a violet humanoid
  silhouette at `0x9D:0x9C`, along with eleven others.  Collision was never the
  objection.  The objection is to a picture that claims to be a particular
  someone and is not.

**adam-bolt -- also modified, with Adam Bolt's own Zangband tiles.**

  Sixty-five of the named characters this project imports were drawn by Adam
  Bolt, for Zangband, for those characters.  Angband's copy of his tileset does
  not carry them; Zangband's does, in `archive/zangband/`.
  `scripts/tiles/import-zangband-tiles.py` copies them into **three new rows**
  at the bottom of `16x16.png` and writes `uniq-ztk.prf`.

  This is neither borrowing nor generating.  It is the tile the artist drew for
  the monster, so it asserts exactly what is true, and DEC-18 permits it for
  that reason.

  The cells could not simply be mapped: the two sheets agree on 390 of 407
  shared monster coordinates, but all 65 of these cells hold different Angband
  art today, because 4.2 reused them.  So pixels were copied, which makes this
  a modification and is why it needed the permission below rather than nothing.

  Nothing already in the sheet is touched -- verified pixel by pixel, rows 0 to
  61 byte-identical -- and the script reclaims its own rows on a re-run.  It
  must be the last generator to run, after make-terrain and make-services.

  And eleven objects, the same way: the magical figurine (3.129.0) and the ten
  statues (3.130.0), which Adam Bolt drew for Zangband and Angband never had.
  They are the cells after the last unique, on the same rows.

**old -- also modified, with Zangband's own 8x8 figurine.**

  Zangband's copy of the 8x8 tileset has a magical figurine and ten statues
  too, and its `graf-xxx.prf` says which cell is which -- `# Magical Figurine`
  above `K:567` -- where for monsters it names almost none.  The same script
  copies those eleven cells into **one new row** at the bottom of `8x8.png` and
  writes `zang-ztk.prf` (3.129.1, and the statues 3.130.0).  The archive's 8x8 tiles have no mask: they sit on a flat grey,
  and the grey is what becomes transparent, which the script's own notes
  calibrate against the cells both sheets share.  Rows 0 to 100 are
  byte-identical.

  Nomad's and Gervais' get nothing: Nomad's is a modern set with no 2001
  counterpart, and Gervais' is mapped-only and staying that way.  So the
  figurine and the statues are their letter in those two, which
  `audit-tiles.py` lists as deliberate.

  Each tileset's own `graf-*.prf` gains `%:` lines loading the above.  That is a
  modification of a text file, not of any art.


**iso -- ours, and not a tileset.**

  `lib/tiles/iso` is the terrain of the Tk front end's isometric map view
  (`src/tcl/iso.c`), drawn by `scripts/tiles/make-iso.py`: three sheets of the
  same drawing in different inks, the letters for its counters, and `iso.txt`,
  which says which drawing each terrain uses.  Nothing in it was taken from
  anybody's art.  The letters are the DS port's 5x8 font, read from
  `src/nds/nds-font-5x8.c`.  ZAngbandTk 2.4's isometric sheet, which was David
  Gervais', is not used: it came with only a permission given to that project.

  It is not in list.txt, because it is not a set of tiles for the flat map,
  and it has no Makefile, because only the Tk front end reads it: CMake
  installs lib/tiles whole, and the Tk packages copy it whole, so it ships
  without an install list.  `make-iso.py --check` is what keeps it honest.

May we?
-------

  * **Adam Bolt's** may be "redistributed and used for any purpose, with or
    without modification".  Explicit.  That covers both modifications above:
    the generated wilderness row, and the sixty-five uniques and the figurine
    imported from Zangband's copy of the same tileset.  Those are his work
    too -- Zangband's changelog records "Adam Bolt's tiles are now finished
    and every monster/object/spell effect has a tile"
    (`archive/zangband/z_update.txt`), so the Zangband rows are the same artist
    under the same terms, not a third party's art travelling under his licence.
  * **David Gervais'** is Creative Commons Attribution 3.0, which permits
    modification with attribution and asks that changes be indicated.  The one
    modification is the generated row described above, and this file is where
    it is indicated.
  * **The original 8x8 and Nomad's** carry no licence statement, here or in
    Angband's own `copying.rst`.  They are not listed among the exceptions in
    LICENSE.md, so they stand on the project licence like the rest of the tree.
    That is a reading of a silence rather than a permission, and it is recorded
    as such rather than assumed.  The figurine added to the 8x8 sheet is the
    same silence one step back: it was drawn for Zangband's copy of the set,
    whose changelog credits its 8x8 additions to Zangband's own contributors
    ("279 new monsters and the corresponding 8x8 sized tiles designed and
    drawed by Topi Ylinen", 2.2.0) and names nobody for this one.  It stands on
    Zangband's licence, as everything else this project took from the archive
    does.
  * **Shockbolt's** was removed in 3.95.0 and is not here.  Its licence grants
    use with Angband and withholds it for "other games or projects", which is
    what this is.

See LICENSE.md and docs/copying.rst for the full terms.


What is still missing
---------------------

Not everything has a tile yet, and the gaps are deliberate rather than
forgotten.  Gervais has no inn, magesmith, chaos tower or recharger; the three
generated sets have none of those, nor a dungeon mouth or a secret door.  Those
are buildings and openings rather than ground textures -- shapes, not rules --
and a borrowed picture for one of them would be actively wrong rather than
merely absent.  They render as ASCII until somebody draws them.  So does the
magical figurine and the statues in the Nomad and Gervais sets, for the same
reason.
